-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292149} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1895e02e-cbf5-58b0-9b56-73c0b66223b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1895e02e-cbf5-58b0-9b56-73c0b66223b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290734} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2981b54-bcf3-5ea2-b4e0-245d9a2cce3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f9fe47a-1d49-50f6-9c5d-ebf149ad2b0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-253873" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | $3$ | {{field-1}} | $\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))}=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $3$ | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-4}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{bmatrix})} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1))} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(1),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ Since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={3}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(1)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) & \\frac{1}{2!}{f}^{″}(1) \\\\ 0 & f(1) & {f}^{′}(1) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{1}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & {3}^{1} & {3}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {3}^{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{(2)}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{({J}_{3}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))} & ={3}^{({J}_{3}(1))}\\,⊕\\,{3}^{({J}_{1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & \\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
